<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#DimensionsAlignment> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Relationships where dimensions mutually reinforce and amplify each other's meaning in memes"@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01497#semiotic_projection_1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The user is not explicitly projected onto any deictic pronoun like 'me' or 'you'. However, the meme's structure and intent create an implicit projection where the viewer (user) is positioned as someone who is either endorsing, mocking, or identifying with the stereotype being presented. The user is projected onto the visual material of the man holding the child, which is then semiotically mapped to the harmful stereotype 'married to children'. This projection is not direct (no 'you' or 'me'), but it is inferable from the meme's function: the user is invited to consume and interpret the image within the context of the stereotype, thereby internalizing or aligning with the meme's framing. The user is projected onto the 'man' visual material, which is the central subject of the stereotype."@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#metaphoricallyMapsTo> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Indicates metaphorical or analogical mapping between dimension instances"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#User> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#DramaticIrony> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "One dimension revealing information another dimension does not provide in memes, e.g. text revealing information visuals don't show"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#Semi-transparentOverlay> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Text that blends with underlying visual content through transparency in memes"@en .
